For $\mathbf{v} \in \dom{\mathbf{V}}$ and $\mathbf{S} \subseteq \mathbf{V}$, we define $Q[\mathbf{S}](\cdot)$ by 
        \begin{equation} Q[\mathbf{S}](\mathbf{v}) := P_{\mathbf{v}\setminus \mathbf{s}}(\mathbf{s}).
        \end{equation}

\textbf{Example 2:}
        Consider again the causal graph in Figure \ref{fig: counterexample}.
        Herein, do-calculus-based methods (e.g., Algorithm \ref{algo: ID}) would report that the causal effect of $\mathbf{X}=\{X_1,X_2\}$ on $\mathbf{Y}=\{Y_1,Y_2\}$ is identifiable given $\mathcal{G}$.
        However, by ignoring the positivity assumption, we can introduce two SEMs $\mathcal{M}_1$ and $\mathcal{M}_2$ in $\mathbb{M}(\mathcal{G})$ that have the same observational distribution but result in two different post-interventional distributions after intervening on $\{X_1,X_2\}$. 
        This clearly contradicts with the identifiability of $P_{x_1,x_2}(y_1,y_2)$.

        All variables in both models are binary. 
        Also, for both models and $i\in \{1,2\}$, we define $P(U_i=0) = P(U_i=1) = 0.5$ and $X_i = U_1$.
        In model $\mathcal{M}_1$, we define $Y_1,Y_2$ to have the following conditional distributions:
        \begin{equation*}
            \begin{split}
                P^{\mathcal{M}_1}(y_1 \mid u_2, x_1) = &\frac{1}{3} \mathds{1}_{y_1=u_2} + \frac{2}{3}\mathds{1}_{y_1\neq u_2},
                \\
                P^{\mathcal{M}_1}(y_2 \mid u_2, x_2) = &\frac{1}{3} \mathds{1}_{y_2 = (u_2\oplus x_2)} + \frac{2}{3}\mathds{1}_{y_2\neq(u_2\oplus x_2)},
            \end{split}
        \end{equation*}
        where $\mathds{1}_{A}$ is the indicator function which is one whenever the statement in $A$ is true and is zero otherwise. 
        For model $\mathcal{M}_2$, we define the conditional distributions of $Y_1,Y_2$ as
        \begin{align*}
                &P^{\mathcal{M}_2}(y_1 \mid u_2, x_1) = \frac{2}{3}\mathds{1}_{y_1=(u_2\oplus x_1)} + \frac{1}{3}\mathds{1}_{y_1\neq(u_2\oplus x_1)},\\
                &P^{\mathcal{M}_2}(y_2|u_2, x_2) = \frac{2}{3}\mathds{1}_{y_2=u_2} + \frac{1}{3}\mathds{1}_{y_2\neq u_2}.
        \end{align*}

        It is straightforward to see that for any realizations $(x_1,x_2,y_1,y_2) \in \dom{\mathbf{V}}$, we have
        \begin{equation*}
            P^{\mathcal{M}_1}(x_1,x_2,y_1,y_2) = P^{\mathcal{M}_2}(x_1,x_2,y_1,y_2).
        \end{equation*}
        However,
        \begin{equation*}
        \begin{split}
            \frac{4}{9}=P^{\mathcal{M}_1}_{x_1=0, x_2=1}(Y_1=0, Y_2=0) \\
            \neq 
            P^{\mathcal{M}_2}_{x_1=0, x_2=1}(Y_1=0, Y_2=0) = \frac{5}{9}.
        \end{split}
        \end{equation*}
        Note that $\mathcal{M}_1$ and $\mathcal{M}_2$ do not belong to $\mathbb{M}^+(\mathcal{G})$, since $P(x_1=0, x_2=1, y_1, y_2)= 0$ for any $y_1\in \dom{Y_1}$ and $y_2\in \dom{Y_2}$.
        This example shows that if we use $\mathbb{M}(\mathcal{G})$ instead of $\mathbb{M}^+(\mathcal{G})$ in Definition \ref{def: id}, the causal effect of $\mathbf{X}$ on $\mathbf{Y}$ is not identifiable from $\mathcal{G}$, and therefore, do-calculus-based methods such as the proposed algorithm in \cite{lee2019general} are not sound.
        Specifically, the proposed algorithm in \cite{lee2019general} suggests the causal effect in this example is g-identifiable and returns the following expression:
        \begin{equation*}
            P_{x_1, x_2}(y_1,, y_2) = P(y_1|x_1, x_2)P(y_2|y_1, x_2, x_1).
        \end{equation*}
        This expression is not well-defined for all realizations ignoring the positivity assumption because for some realizations $P(x_1, x_2)$ is zero which means the conditional distribution $P(y_1|x_1, x_2)$ is not well-defined.
        Thus, the algorithm in \cite{lee2019general} \textit{is not sound}.

Suppose Algorithm \ref{algo: GID} determines that the causal effect of $\mathbf{X}$ on $\mathbf{Y}$ is g-identifiable from $(\mathbb{A}, \mathcal{G})$. 
    Analogous to the method in \cite{tian2003ID}, we can derive a formula for $P_{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{Y})$ as follows.
    For each $\mathbf{S}_i \in \{\mathbf{S}_1,\cdots,\mathbf{S}_l\}$, we can derive a formula for $Q[\mathbf{S}_i]$ using \textbf{ID\_Single} function in line $4$ of \textbf{GID\_Single}. 
    This allows us to compute $Q[\mathbf{S}]$ using 
    \begin{equation*}
        Q[\mathbf{S}] = \prod_{i=1}^l Q[\mathbf{S}_i].
    \end{equation*}
    Finally, the expression for $P_{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{Y})$ will be
    \begin{equation*}
        P_{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{Y}) = \sum_{\mathbf{S}\setminus \mathbf{Y}} Q[\mathbf{S}].
    \end{equation*}

\begin{split}
        Q^{\mathcal{M}_1}[\mathbf{A}'_i](\mathbf{v}) 
        = \sum_{j=1}^d \frac{1}{d} \theta_{i,j}(\mathbf{v}), \\
        Q^{\mathcal{M}_2}[\mathbf{A}'_i](\mathbf{v}) 
